    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Layer Confusion:</strong> Confusing the Network layer (IP addresses) with the Data Link layer (MAC addresses).</li>
            <li><strong>IDS vs. IPS:</strong> Assuming an IDS can block traffic; students must understand that an IDS only alerts, while an IPS acts.</li>
            <li><strong>Proxy Directions:</strong> Confusing a forward Proxy (protects the client) with a Reverse Proxy (protects the server).</li>
            <li><strong>Routing Logic:</strong> Thinking routers always use the shortest physical path; they use the "optimal" path based on congestion, cost, or speed.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Classification of Knowledge:</strong> Does the 4-layer TCP/IP model reflect the "truth" of electricity and light, or is it a human-made abstraction designed purely for utility?</li>
                <li><strong>Expertise and Trust:</strong> How do digital certificates and SSL/TLS change our "belief" in the identity of a remote server?</li>
            </ul>
